<exercises xml:id="sol-1-5-Metric_system_and_scientific_notation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<xi:include href="ex-intro.ptx" />

<page>
	<exercise xml:id="sol-gdp-scientific-notation">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In 2011,
				<aside>
					<p>
						Source: U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis, U.S. Census Bureau
					</p>
					<!-- <p>
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="gdp-prelude-scientific-notation" />)
					</p> -->
				</aside>
				the GDP (gross domestic product) of the United States was approximately $15,596 billion in 2011 and the population of the United States was approximately 0.313 billion that year. 
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Writing the population as 0.313 billion seems strange. A more natural unit would be millions. Rewrite the population in millions of people.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						0.313\ \text{billion} \ast
						\frac{1{,}000{,}000{,}000}{1\ \text{billion}} \ast
						\frac{1\ \text{million}}{1{,}000{,}000}=
						\frac{0.313\cdot 10^9}{10^6} = 313\ \text{million}
					</md>
					
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Rewrite the population in people, both in normal decimal notation (that means with all the 0s) and in scientific notation.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						0.313\ \text{billion} \ast 
						\frac{1{,}000{,}000{,}000}{1\ \text{billion}} = 
						313{,}000{,}000 = 3.13 \times 10^8
					</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					It also seems strange to write the GDP as $15,596 billion. A more natural unit would be <term>trillions</term>, where 1 trillion = 1,000,000,000,000. Rewrite the GDP in trillions of dollars.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						\$15{,}596\ \text{billion} \ast
						\frac{1{,}000{,}000{,}000}{1\ \text{billion}} \ast
						\frac{1\ \text{trillion}}{1{,}000{,}000{,}000{,}000} = 
						\frac{15{,}596 \cdot 10^9}{1 \cdot 10^{12}} 
						=\$ 15.596\ \text{trillion}
					</md>
					
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Rewrite the GDP in dollars, both in normal decimal notation and in scientific notation.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						\$15{,}596\ \text{billion} \ast
						\frac{1{,}000{,}000{,}000}{1\ \text{billion}} =
						15{,}596{,}000{,}000{,}000
						= 1.5596 \times 10^{13}
					</md>
					
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Calculate the GDP <term>per capita</term> (meaning per person) by dividing the GDP in dollars by the population in people. Express your answer in $/person.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						\frac{\$15{,}596{,}000{,}000{,}000}{313{,}000{,}000\ \text{people}}
						\approx \$49{,}827 / \text{person} 
						\approx \$50{,}000/\text{person}.
					</md>
					
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					For practice, repeat your calculation using the numbers in scientific notation.
					<aside>
						<p>Because <m>\times</m> and <m>\div</m> are at the same level in the order of operations, you need to put parentheses around each number in scientific notation before dividing.</p>
					</aside>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						\frac{(1.5596 \times 10^{13})}{(3.13 \times 10^8)}
						\approx 49{,}827\ldots \checkmark
					</md>
					
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</page>

<page>
	<exercise xml:id="sol-dust-particle-scientific-notation">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Edgar recently changed the cleaning bag on his vacuum cleaner. 
				<!-- <aside>
					<p>
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="dust-particle-prelude-scientific-notation" />)
					</p>
				</aside> -->
				He became curious about how many particles of dust were in the bag. Edgar did a little research online and found out that the mass of a dust particle is <me>0.000~000~000~753\text{ kilograms.}</me>(The strange-looking spaces are to help you see that there are 9 zeros in the number.)
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write the mass of a dust particle in scientific notation.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Since the decimal point is 10 places to the left of where it needs to be, the mass of a dust particle is <m>7.53\times 10^{-10}</m> kg.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Recall that
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\amp\textbf{kilo} \amp
							\amp= \amp 
							\amp 1 \text{ thousand} \amp
							\amp =\amp
							1{,}000\amp\amp
							\amp=\amp
							10^3\amp
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp\textbf{milli} \amp
							\amp= \amp 
							\amp 1 \text{ in a thousand} \amp
							\amp =\amp
							0\amp.001\amp
							\amp=\amp
							10^{-3}\amp
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp\textbf{micro} \amp
							\amp= \amp 
							\amp 1 \text{ in a million} \amp
							\amp =\amp
							0\amp.000~001\amp
							\amp=\amp
							10^{-6}\amp
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp\textbf{nano} \amp
							\amp= \amp 
							\amp 1 \text{ in a billion} \amp
							\amp =\amp
							0\amp.000~000~001\amp
							\amp=\amp
							10^{-9}\amp
						</mrow>
					</md>
					Express the mass of a dust particle in each of the given units:
				</p>
			</introduction>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						grams
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<md>
							7.53\times 10^{-10}\ \cancel{\text{kg}} \ast
							\frac{1000\ \text{g}}{1 \ \cancel{\text{kg}}}
							= 7.53 \times 10^{-10} \times 1000 = 
							0.000~000~753\ \text{g}
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						milligrams (mg)
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<md>
							0.000~000~753\ \cancel{\text{g}}\ast
							\frac{1000\ \text{mg}}{1\ \cancel{\text{g}}}
							= 0.000~753\ \text{mg}
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						micrograms (<m>\mu</m>g)
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<md>
							0.000~000~753\ \cancel{\text{g}}\ast
							\frac{1{,}000{,}000\ \mu\text{g}}{1\ \cancel{\text{g}}}
							= 0.753\ \mu\text{g}
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						nanograms (ng)
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<md>
							0.000~000~753\ \cancel{\text{g}}\ast
							\frac{1{,}000{,}000{,}000\ \text{ng}}{1\ \cancel{\text{g}}}
							= 753\ \text{ng}
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Edgar determined that the full vacuum bag weighed 5 pounds. How many dust particles were in the bag? (I am already sneezing.) Use <m>1 \text{ kilogram} \approx 2.2 \text{ pounds}</m>. Express your answer in scientific notation.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\amp 5\ \cancel{\text{pounds}} \ast 
							\frac{1\ \cancel{\text{kg}}}{2.2\ \cancel{\text{pounds}}} \ast
							\frac{1\ \text{dust particle}}{7.53\times 10^{-10}\ \cancel{\text{kg}}}
						</mrow>
						<mrow> \amp= \frac{5\cdot 1\cdot 1}{2.2\cdot 7.53\times 10^{-10}} </mrow>
						<mrow> \amp=3.108\ldots \times 10^9 \approx 3\times 10^{9} = 3{,}000{,}000{,}000 </mrow>
						<mrow> \amp= 3\ \text{billion dust particles (!!)} </mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</page>

<page>
	<exercise xml:id="sol-planet-weight-scientific-notation">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				The list shows the (approximate) mass of the planets in our solar system.
				<aside>
					<p>
						Source: Wikipedia (Solar System)
					</p>
					<!-- <p>
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="earth-weight-prelude-scientific-notation"/>)
					</p> -->
				</aside>
			</p>
			<tabular>
			<row>
				<cell halign="left">Earth</cell>
				<cell halign="left"><m>5.972 \times 10^{24}</m> kg</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell halign="left">Jupiter</cell>
				<cell halign="left"><m>1.899 \times 10^{27}</m> kg</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell halign="left">Mars</cell>
				<cell halign="left"><m>6.417 \times 10^{23}</m> kg</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell halign="left">Mercury</cell>
				<cell halign="left"><m>3.302 \times 10^{23}</m> kg</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell halign="left">Neptune</cell>
				<cell halign="left"><m>1.024 \times 10^{26}</m> kg</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell halign="left">Saturn</cell>
				<cell halign="left"><m>5.685 \times 10^{26}</m> kg</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell halign="left">Uranus</cell>
				<cell halign="left"><m>8.681 \times 10^{25}</m> kg</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell halign="left">Venus</cell>
				<cell halign="left"><m>4.868 \times 10^{24}</m> kg</cell>
			</row>
			</tabular>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write the mass of Earth and the mass of Mars in standard decimal notation. Which is heavier?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\text{Earth: } 5{,} 972\amp{,}000{,}000{,}000{,}000{,}000{,}000{,}000\ \text{kg} </mrow>
						<mrow>\text{Mars: }       641\amp{,}700{,}000{,}000{,}000{,}000{,}000{,}000\ \text{kg} </mrow>
					</md>
					Earth is heavier. (Almost 10 times heavier!)
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					List the planets from heaviest (largest mass) to lightest (smallest mass).
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Look for the higher exponents on the 10 in scientific notation first, and then look at the numbers. 
				</p>
				<p>
					From heaviest to lightest: Jupiter, Saturn, Neptune, Uranus, Earth, Venus, Mars, and Mercury.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The mass of astronomical bodies are sometimes measured in <term>Jupiter mass</term>, abbreviated <m>M_J</m>, where <m>1 M_J = 1.899 \times 10^{27}</m> kg. 
					<aside>
						<p>Because <m>\times</m> and <m>\div</m> are at the same level in the order of operations, you need to put parentheses around each number in scientific notation before dividing.</p>
					</aside>
					Express Earth's mass in <m>M_J</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\text{Earth }\amp= 5.972\times 10^{24}\ \cancel{\text{kg}} \ast
							\frac{1\ M_J}{1.899\times 10^{27}\ \cancel{\text{kg}}} 
						</mrow>
						<mrow> \amp = \frac{5.972\times 10^{24}}{1.899\times 10^{27}}</mrow>
						<mrow> \amp = 0.00314 \ldots \approx 0.003 M_J</mrow>
					</md>
					
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</page>

<page>
	<exercise xml:id="sol-iv-drip-scientific-notation">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Souksavanh is setting up a patient's intravenous (IV) medication. 
				<!-- <aside>
					<p component="not-workbook">
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="iv-drip-approx-rounding"/>)
					</p>
					<p component="workbook">
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="sec-0-1-Approximation_and_rounding" text="global"/> Exercises)
					</p>
				</aside> -->
				She sets the drip at 42 drops/minute. The drip chamber size is 20 drops/mL. Recall
				<md>
					<mrow>
						\amp\textbf{milli} \amp
						\amp= \amp 
						\amp 1 \text{ in a thousand} \amp
						\amp =\amp
						0\amp.001\amp
						\amp=\amp
						10^{-3}\amp
					</mrow>
					<mrow>
						\amp\textbf{micro} \amp
						\amp= \amp 
						\amp 1 \text{ in a million} \amp
						\amp =\amp
						0\amp.000~001\amp
						\amp=\amp
						10^{-6}\amp
					</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					At what rate is the IV fluid being delivered to Souk's patient? Answer in mL/min (millileters per minute).
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						\frac{42\ \cancel{\text{drops}}}{1\ \text{min}} \ast
						\frac{1\ \text{mL}}{20\ \cancel{\text{drops}}} =
						\frac{42\cdot 1}{1\cdot 20} = 2.1\ \text{mL / min}
					</md>
					
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					How fast is the drip measured in <m>\mu</m>L/sec (microliters per second)?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						\frac{2.1\ \cancel{\text{mL}}}{1\ \cancel{\text{min}}} \ast
						\frac{1\ \cancel{\text{min}}}{60\ \text{sec}} \ast
						\frac{1\ \cancel{\text{L}}}{1000\ \cancel{\text{mL}}} \ast
						\frac{1{,}000{,}000\ \mu\text{L}}{1\ \cancel{\text{L}}} 
						= 
						\frac{2.1\cdot 1 \cdot 1\cdot 1{,}000{,}000}{1\cdot 60 \cdot 1000 \cdot 1}
						= 35\ \mu\text{L / sec}
					</md>
					
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					If the drip bag holds 1 liter, how long will it take the drip to run? Express your answer in hours and minutes.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					First let's convert 1 liter into minutes:
					<aside>
						<p>See how we used part (a)?</p>
					</aside>
					<md>
						1\ \cancel{\text{L}} \ast
						\frac{1000\ \cancel{\text{mL}}}{1\ \cancel{\text{L}}} \ast
						\frac{1\ \text{min}}{2.1\ \cancel{\text{mL}}} =
						\frac{1\cdot 1000 \cdot 1}{1\cdot 2.1} = 476.19\ldots \approx 476\ \text{min}
					</md>
					Now let's convert 476 minutes into hours and minutes:
					<md>
						476\ \cancel{\text{min}} \ast
						\frac{1\ \text{hr}}{60\ \cancel{\text{min}}} = 
						\frac{476}{60} = 7.933\ldots\ \text{hr}
					</md>
					<md>
						0.933\ \cancel{\text{hr}} \ast
						\frac{60\ \text{min}}{1\ \cancel{\text{hr}}} =
						0.933 \cdot 60 \approx 56\ \text{min}
					</md>
					So the drip will take 7:56 (7 hours and 56 minutes) to run.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The concentration of medication is 1.7 mg/mL (milligrams per milliliter). How much medication is in the 1 liter bag? Convert your answer to grams. Explain what you notice.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						1\ \cancel{\text{L}} \ast
						\frac{1000\ \cancel{\text{mL}}}{1\ \cancel{\text{L}}} \ast
						\frac{1.7\ \cancel{\text{mg}}}{1\ \cancel{\text{mL}}} \ast
						\frac{1\ \text{g}}{1000 \ \cancel{\text{mg}}} =
						\frac{1\cdot 1000 \cdot 1.7\cdot 1}{1\cdot 1\cdot 1000} = 
						1.7\ \text{grams}
					</md>
					Hey, same number! It's like the <q>m</q> for <q>micro</q> cancelled out.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					At what rate is the medication being delivered to Souk's patient? Answer in g/min (grams per minute).
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The 1 liter bag holds 1.7 grams of medication and takes 476 minutes to empty:
					<md>\frac{1.7\ \text{g}}{476\ \text{min}} \approx 0.0035\ \text{g / min}</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</page>

	

	<exercise xml:id="sol-file-size-scientific-notation" component="not-workbook">
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					How many files at an average of 42.3 MB each can each gig (1 GB) of computer memory hold?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Tara's coworker Brandon has a much faster Internet connection on his computer at 1,500 kbps. How long would it take Brandon to download 700 MB?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					At that rate, how much information could Brandon download in 8 hours? Express your answer in kilobytes (KB).
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="sol-billion-seconds-scientific-notation" component="not-workbook">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				<em>(Story also appears in <xref ref="billion-seconds-prelude-scientific-notation"/>)</em>
			</p>
			<aside>
				<p>
					Source: Mathew Foss, North Hennepin Community College
				</p>
			</aside>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Convert each of these amounts of time into an understandable unit of time: 1 million seconds, 1 billion seconds, 1 trillion seconds.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Billy Bob wants to throw a party when he turns 1 billion seconds old. About how many years old will he be?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<em>Bonus question:</em> On what date were you or will you be 1 billion seconds old? Don't forget leap years!
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="sol-proton-mass-scientific-notation" component="not-workbook">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				A proton has mass of about <m>1.67262 \times 10^{-27}</m> kg, 
				<aside>
					<p>
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="proton-mass-prelude-scientific-notation"/>)
					</p>
				</aside>
				while an electron has mass of about <m>9.10938 \times 10^{-31}</m> kg.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write out the mass of a proton and that of an electron in normal decimal notation.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which is heavier (has greater mass)?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					How many times heavier is it? To calculate the answer take the mass of the heavier particle and divide it by the mass of the lighter particle.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					How many protons would it take to weigh an ounce? Use <m>1 \text{ ounce} \approx 28.3 \text{ grams}</m> and, as always, 1 kg = 1,000 grams. <em>Because <m>\times</m> and <m>\div</m> are at the same level in the order of operations, you should put parentheses around each number in scientific notation before dividing.</em>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="sol-serving-size-scientific-notation" component="not-workbook">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				How many servings are in:
			</p>
		</introduction>	
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A 2-liter bottle of a soft drink where the serving size is 250 mL?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A 750 mL bottle of wine where a serving size is 5 (fluid) ounces? Use <m>1 \text{ quart} = 32 \text{ (fluid) ounces}</m> and <m>1 \text{ liter} \approx 1.056 \text{ quarts}</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="sol-cells-scientific-notation" component="not-workbook">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Rayka weighs 140 pounds. 
				<aside>
					<p>
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="cells-prelude-scientific-notation"/>)
					</p>
				</aside>
				She would like to approximate how many cells are in her body. Use the following information: <m>1 \text{ cell} \approx 1 \times 10^{-15} \text{ g}</m>, <m>1 \text{ kg} \approx 2.2 \text{ pounds}</m>, and, as always, 1 kg = 1,000 g.
			</p>
		</introduction>	
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					How many cells are in Rayka's body? Write your answer in scientific notation.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Rewrite your answer in the most appropriate unit: millions (<m>10^6</m>), billions (<m>10^9</m>), trillions (<m>10^{12}</m>), quadrillions (<m>10^{15}</m>), or quintillions (<m>10^{18}</m>).
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="sol-bmi-scientific-notation" component="not-workbook">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				<term>Body Mass Index</term> (BMI) is one indicator of whether a person is a healthy weight. 
				<aside>
					<p>
						Source: Centers for Disease Control and Prevention
					</p>
					<p>
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="bmi-approx-rounding"/>)
					</p>
				</aside>
				BMI values between 18.5 and 24.9 are considered <q>normal</q>. Jared is 6<prime/>4<dblprime/> and weighs 200 pounds. He would like to calculate his BMI from this guide: <me>\text{BMI} = \text{weight in kilograms} \div \text{height in meters} \wedge 2</me>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Check that Jared is around 1.93 meters tall and weighs around 90.91 kilograms. Use <m>1 \text{ inch} \approx 2.54 \text{ cm}</m> and <m>1 \text{ kilogram} \approx 2.2 \text{ pounds}</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Jared entered the following keystrokes on his calculator: <me>90.91 \div 1.93 \wedge 2=</me> and got the answer <me>\text{Jared's BMI } = 24.4060243\ldots</me> Is his BMI considered <q>normal</q>?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose Jared had rounded off his height to 1.9 meters and his weight to 91 kilograms. Calculate his BMI by entering the following keystrokes your calculator: <me>91 \div 1.9 \wedge 2=</me> What do you get? Round your answer to one decimal place. Is Jared's BMI considered <q>normal</q>?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					What would you tell Jared?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</exercises>
